# DESCRIPTION

mali drives an Arm Mali GPU of the command-stream (CSF) generations: it powers the GPU up, resets it, gives it its page tables and boots the firmware of its microcontroller (the MCU) until the firmware answers a ping.
devmgr(8) starts it for the CIX Sky1's GPU:

```text
driver mali
class gpu
match acpi CIXH5000
own-mmu
coherence noncoherent CIX runs it on ACE-Lite, uncached (AbyssBSD gpu.md)
power-up graph
power-clocks gpu_clk_core gpu_clk_stacks
reset-pulse 20
firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin
```

The GPU translates through page tables mali writes, so `own-mmu` gives it the BTI of a device behind no IOMMU whatever the IORT names, and the kernel warns of it.
Its `_CCA` says coherent and the GPU isn't, as CIX's own kernel treats it (the display controller doesn't snoop), so the record's `coherence` wins and mali is started with `--noncoherent`.
`vx hardware trust cix-sky1` lists it as trusted with all of memory.

The Sky1's GPU answers nothing until its power-up is done, in this order, and a read of its registers before it stops the machine (an SError, then a power cycle).
devmgr(8) does the first four steps before it starts the driver, since the record says `power-up graph` (driver(6)), each through what owns it:

1. its power domain, SCMI domain 21 (`_DSD`'s `power-domains`), turned on through scmi(3);
2. its clocks `gpu_clk_core` and `gpu_clk_stacks` (`CLKT`: SCMI ids 31 and 32) turned on the same way;
3. its power resource's `_ON`, which devmgr runs;
4. its reset, `gpu_reset` in `RSTL` (index 9 on the S5 reset controller), pulsed through the controller's driver, sky1-reset(3): held for 20 µs and let go, and given 20 µs more (the record's `reset-pulse 20`: the GPU needs the line asserted and let go, not only released).

The driver starts with the GPU on and does the rest, in its own registers, in this order.
A step that fails stops it there: the steps after it are not taken, the status says which failed and why, and the GPU's fault, MCU and interrupt registers at that moment.
The waits are polls of the raw interrupt status with every interrupt masked, each with a limit.

5. Q-channel clock gating turned on, bit 0 of its RCSU's (its first range's) register `0x218`.
6. The probe, from its second range: GPU_ID, the L2, tiler, memory and MMU features, the address spaces, the CSF id, GPU_FEATURES and the shader core, tiler and L2 present masks, and the GPU's name as Arm gives it (`Mali-G720-Immortalis`: architecture 12, product 0, ten cores or more, with ray intersection). A GPU_ID of 0 or all ones, or before the 10th architecture, is no GPU.
7. The harvest: the RCSU's word at `0x304` says which shader cores are fused off, and the shader mask is the GPU's own `SHADER_PRESENT` less those (of the part's ten cores, at bits 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 16, 18, 20 and 22). Every later mask is the reduced one.
8. A soft reset, then every interrupt block masked and cleared; RESET_COMPLETED within 100 ms.
9. COHERENCY_PROTOCOL set to ACE-Lite, and read back, with the shareable-cache bit where COHERENCY_FEATURES has it.
10. The system cache's values: SYSC_PBHA_OVERRIDE3 `0x22000000` and SYSC_ALLOC0 to SYSC_ALLOC7.
11. The L2 powered on, slice 0 only (the firmware powers the shader cores and tilers), L2_READY within 20 ms.
12. The MCU's address space: page tables in the GPU's format (AArch64 stage 1, 4 KiB pages and 2 MiB blocks, as many levels as MMU_FEATURES' VA bits need) in write-combining pinned memory, and each of the firmware's sections in pinned memory of its own, write-combining too, mapped at its address with the permissions its flags give. Nothing the GPU reads is cached by the CPU, so the CPU does no cache maintenance, and no section is mapped cacheable on the GPU's side. The initial bytes are copied in and the rest zeroed where the section says so.
13. Address space 0 programmed with those tables (an UPDATE, within 100 ms; its attributes and translation configuration are the non-coherent ones, since the record says `coherence noncoherent`).
14. MCU_CONTROL set to auto, and the firmware's interrupt for the global interface (bit 31 of the job interrupt block) within 1 s.
15. The interface discovered in the firmware's shared section: its version, the CSG and CS slots, each checked to lie inside the section and to agree with the others.
16. The global interface configured (every shader core allowed, the power-off timer at 10 ms and the idle timer at 800 µs of GPU_TIMESTAMP, whose rate it measures over 2 ms, the progress timer, the idle and counter bits) and a ping, which the firmware must acknowledge within 100 ms. The configuration's acknowledge is waited for 100 ms too, but only reported.

Its record names the CSF firmware, `firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin` (driver(6), firmware(6)), so devmgr gives its host that one file at `/lib/firmware/arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin`, read only.
On start mali reads it and checks its header and every section against the format's rules; an image that breaks one is refused whole and the status names the rule.
Sections for the protected mode are left out.
Where the image has no firmware (the cache was empty when the board's image was built), the GPU is probed and the status says `no firmware`.

The GPU is left powered with its firmware running and its address space 0 programmed.
Its job, MMU and GPU interrupts stay masked: nothing is served yet that needs them.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/mali-acpi-_SB_.GPU_`.

: `status`
  Read only.
  The firmware first: `firmware <size> bytes, <n> sections, git <revision>` (no revision when the image has none), `firmware <size> bytes refused: <rule>`, or `no firmware`.
  Then a line or more for each step done, in the order above, of these kinds:
  `Q-channel clock gating on, RCSU control <hex> harvest <hex>`;
  the GPU's name and GPU_ID and its fields (`Mali-G720-Immortalis, GPU_ID 0xc8700008: architecture 12.8.7, r0p0 status 8`), its cores (`shader cores 10 (0x00550555), tiler 0x00000001, L2 0x00000001`), the feature registers, and `probed`;
  `harvest: ...`, `soft reset: ...`, `coherency: ...`, `system cache: ...`, `L2 powered: ...`, `MCU VM: ...`, `firmware loaded: ...`, `address space 0: ...` and `MCU booted: ...`, each with the values it wrote or waited for;
  the firmware's interface: `firmware interface <major>.<minor>.<patch>, GLB_FEATURES <hex>`, then its slots: `<n> CSG slots (...), <m> CS slots each (CS_FEATURES <hex>: <r> work registers, <s> scoreboards, <kinds>)`;
  `GPU_TIMESTAMP counts at <hz> Hz`, `global interface configured: ...` and `ping answered, GLB_ACK <hex>`.
  On success the last lines are `budget name=gpu.mali.boot_ms value=<ms> unit=ms`, the time from the host's start to the firmware's answer to the ping, and `firmware booted`.
  Where a step failed, the last line is `FAILED at <step>: <why>`, the step one of `Q-channel clock gating`, `probe`, `firmware`, `harvest`, `soft reset`, `coherency`, `L2 power`, `MCU VM`, `firmware load`, `address space 0`, `MCU boot`, `interface` or `ping`.
  A GPU that was probed and then failed in a later step has no `firmware booted`.

